one doesn't think of mount charleston  without noting griffith peak and mary  jane falls both named for the  prestigious family who helped shape las  vegas into the bustling city it is today  and developed much of kyle canyon in the  early 1900s here's a look back at its  beginnings and some historic photos  thanks to bob stoldahl the chairman of  the city of las vegas historic  preservation board  well i worked there i remember some of  the people that came up during the time  i worked there and the romances that  took place bob stold started collecting  old photos of the earlier years of mount  charleston lodge when he started working  as a janitor there in 1961. when i  graduated from las vegas high school it  was an opportunity to learn to become a  bartender he says mount charleston as a  recreational facility really began in  1905 after e.w griffith and his family  created griffith's camp which over the  years expanded into a dude ranch where  people would stay to get their divorce  dude ranches were really just a place  where you could spend your six weeks  that you had to have quote residency in  in las vegas at one point it was known  as charleston park then the charleston  park lodge and casino and at one point  the casino was even called the ponderosa  post world war ii that the frontier  hotel leaked the entire area at least  the lodge and the cabin and they were  successful because they knew how to run  a hotel casino and take care of visitors  and and all of that that was in  1948 and they operated for for a while  then there was a change of hands through  all that los vegas were drawn to the  wonderfulness of mount charleston but in  1961 a fire destroyed the lodge the  owners of the hacienda resort owned the  lodge at the time and were determined to  rebuild construction began in 1962 and  several years later the restaurant and  lounge were added the orkut family took  over operations and in 1994 added more  than a dozen cabins in 2018 it was sold  to the ellis family which also owned the  ellis island casino in las vegas  many valley residents of course have  such fond memories of the lodge please  share them with us you can send your  photos to pixset's pix  8newsnow.com we've been sharing some of  these thank you kathy here's just a few  that you and kimberly here are sharing  with us today
 

